         <t indent="0" pn="section-1.4-1">The XML vocabulary here is defined in prose, based on the RELAX NG
-schema <xref target="RNC" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RNC"/> contained in Appendix C (specified in RELAX NG Compact
+schema <xref target="RNC" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RNC"/> contained in <xref target="section-appendix.c" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Appendix C"/> (specified in RELAX NG Compact
 Notation (RNC)).</t>
         <t indent="0" pn="section-1.4-2">Note that the schema can be used for automated validity checks, but
 certain constraints are only described in prose (example: the
@@ -1294,7 +1207,7 @@
 characters <xref target="USASCII" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="USASCII"/>.  Thus, the character repertoire consists of
 &quot;A-Z&quot;, &quot;a-z&quot;, &quot;0-9&quot;, &quot;_&quot;, &quot;-&quot;, &quot;.&quot;, and &quot;:&quot;, where &quot;0-9&quot;, &quot;.&quot;, and
 &quot;-&quot; are disallowed as start characters.  Anchors are described in
-more detail in Appendix B.2.</t>
+more detail in <xref target="section-b.2" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Appendix B.2"/>.</t>
       <t indent="0" pn="section-2-4">Tools interpreting the XML described here will collapse horizontal
 whitespace and line breaks to a single whitespace (except inside
 &lt;artwork&gt; and &lt;sourcecode&gt;) and will trim leading and trailing
@@ -1310,105 +1223,77 @@
 renderers that display different fonts for ideographic characters
